Gateway configuration comparison availability
=============================================

The legacy comparison endpoint now requires both a successful current-content
result and a successful history result containing nonempty baseline text.
Failed or malformed sources return fixed HTTP 502 errors; explicitly unsupported
sources return 501; an absent backup or absent backup contents returns 409.
These outcomes never contain has_changes:false or an empty success diff.
Complete comparisons include comparison_available:true and retain their existing
unified diff, size and summary fields. Device diagnostic text is not included in
the new comparison errors. Both child and parent cancellation propagate, even
when the sibling retrieval also fails. Existing admission and adapter cleanup
remain in place; broader adapter logging is not changed by this boundary.

The backup UI requires a complete comparison response, consistent boolean and
diff-line fields, and positive document sizes before displaying a result.
Missing/malformed data or a failed request shows comparison unavailable; a
refresh hides the old result while pending. A failed refresh does not display
stale success. Retry is explicit and performs no backup or device write.

This establishes availability truth, not a new backup engine. Normalized vendor
backup metadata does not itself supply a baseline. In particular, the current
OPNsense export is its latest device backup, not a separate running-state proof;
see OPNSENSE-CONFIG-DOWNLOAD. A provider needs real, correctly identified content
for both sides before this endpoint can establish a meaningful comparison.
This change does not implement durable configuration capture, historical-content
retrieval, backup-trigger persistence, restoration or vendor-wide live drift.
It does not add caller or resource reauthorization after device I/O, bound all
possible diff costs, or sanitize arbitrary adapter logs.

Qualification covers actual encrypted PostgreSQL rows, HTTP routes and service
outcomes with synthetic authenticated identities and adapter responses. The UI
tests mount the real component. Hardware, vendor transport, backup storage and
restore behavior are not proven by these fixtures. No schema or dependency
changes occur.
